WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.175
And now I'd like to introduce you to our featured presenter today,

00:00:14.175 --> 00:00:16.680
uh, Professor Julia Novy-Hildesley.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.230
So Julia is a Professor of the Practice and

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.505
the Executive Director of Stanford's Change Leadership for Sustainability Program.

00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:27.990
Her research and teaching focuses on business strategies, leadership approaches,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.320
and cross-sector partnerships that spur global development and

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.160
align systems with the goal of intergenerational well-being.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.359
Prior to Stanford, Julia served as the Executive Director of the Lemelson Foundation,

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:44.205
the Director of World Wildlife Fund's Pacific Marine Office,

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:46.635
and the CEO of Washington STEM.

00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:49.470
Julia holds a BA from Stanford University

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.345
and a Masters in Philosophy from the University of Sussex,

00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:53.720
where she was recognized as

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.585
a distinguished Young Global Leader by the World Economic Forum in 2010.

00:00:58.585 --> 00:01:02.325
And with that, let us turn it over to Julia.

00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:05.205
Hello and thank you for joining us.

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:06.980
I'm really thrilled to have all of you here

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.695
today and to talk about such an important topic,

00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:11.825
um, so important in our time,

00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:13.580
and really looking forward to the Q&amp;A at the end,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.380
as well to hear some of the perspectives all of

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.450
you have regarding this shift we're starting to

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.880
see in business as it becomes more and more of the norm to embrace sustainability.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.840
So today we're going to organize our conversation,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.080
uh, around three core themes. There we go.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.540
Okay, so first, why are we seeing this shift in approach?

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.834
Why are we seeing more and more businesses,

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:39.660
and organizations in general, embracing sustainability?

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:46.355
Second, uh, what strategic changes are organizations making as they embrace this change?

00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:49.430
And finally, how are they actually implementing these changes?

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.335
So we're gonna look a little bit at the why,

00:01:51.335 --> 00:01:54.695
and the what, and the how, throughout the hour.

00:01:54.695 --> 00:01:57.810
Um, first let me share a perspective,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.575
um, as we [LAUGHTER] get into the why here.

00:02:01.575 --> 00:02:05.270
You know, what is causing organizations to increasingly embrace sustainability?

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.715
And Paul Polman, former CEO of Unilever, uh,

00:02:07.715 --> 00:02:09.185
where he was for a decade,

00:02:09.185 --> 00:02:12.380
summarizes an insight that is driving the shift.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:13.590
And fundamentally, he says,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.490
"We must develop a business model aimed at contributing to

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.585
society and environment instead of taking from them."

00:02:19.585 --> 00:02:23.000
Um, and it's not just Unilever that has this insight of course,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.365
we're seeing organizations and businesses of all varieties,

00:02:26.365 --> 00:02:31.670
from small companies to transnationals like Unilever, embracing sustainability.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.430
And not simply as we've seen in the past as an effort to comply with regulations,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:41.720
or to be charitable through corporate social responsibility initiatives, but really,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.135
um, embracing sustainability as core strategy,

00:02:44.135 --> 00:02:47.750
putting sustainability at the heart of a company's business model.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:52.700
So let's step back a little bit and ask ourselves why are our businesses doing this,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.230
why is this happening,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.990
and why is it happening now.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:00.050
As we've all experienced firsthand in our personal lives and in our work, um,

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:04.775
we are increasingly operating in an environment characterized by volatility,

00:03:04.775 --> 00:03:08.535
uncertainty, complexity, and ambiguity.

00:03:08.535 --> 00:03:11.870
And VUCA is- is actually a term that was coined by

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.999
the US Army War College to describe

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:17.420
the geopolitical context resulting from the end of the Cold War.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.530
But more recently, it is now used widely in business

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.350
to describe the complexity of the operating environment, um, that we're in.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.650
So we could of course devote an entire course to the causes of VUCA World,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.360
but in a nutshell,

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.610
VUCA World results from

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.180
the fundamentally dynamic and nonlinear interactions

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.940
between human beings and the natural environment.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.510
So as we're going about our business,

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.070
we are working and living in a vol- a natural environment,

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:52.085
and there are all sorts of feedbacks and interactions that are so complex and non-linear,

00:03:52.085 --> 00:03:55.825
that it leads to a lot of this volatility and- and complexity that we're seeing.

00:03:55.825 --> 00:03:58.065
And from the human side, we're looking at, you know,

00:03:58.065 --> 00:04:00.140
the combination of technology breakthroughs across

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.495
a wide variety of fields that had- have impact,

00:04:02.495 --> 00:04:04.265
population growth of course,

00:04:04.265 --> 00:04:07.190
depletion of natural resources, and climate change,

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:09.920
and also an increasing demand by

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.420
the public for transparency and accountability in business.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:20.680
So all of this is feeding into businesses shifting their approach,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.140
um, to one where sustainability is more embraced.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.360
And, um, what we're starting to see is

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.955
that because of the volatility companies are experiencing,

00:04:28.955 --> 00:04:34.010
they began to look outward in an effort to understand the sources of disruption may face,

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.515
and to determine how best to navigate them.

00:04:36.515 --> 00:04:41.214
So this has led many institutions to take more of a systems approach,

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:45.050
recognizing their fundamental interconnectedness to the world around them,

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:49.280
and see not only the extent to which they are impacted by outside forces,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.590
but becoming more and more aware of the breadth and depth of

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.060
their own impact on society and the natural environment.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.720
Dr. Paul Polman at Unilever, um,

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.100
he talks about the need for companies to see themselves

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.915
as part of an interconnected web linking multiple players.

00:05:04.915 --> 00:05:08.480
And taking this as perspective as companies do this,

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.900
it naturally deepens the responsibility companies feel

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.160
and organizations feel towards diverse stakeholders as they become more aware.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.990
Um, and also, um,

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.040
it becomes clear that without engagement and collaboration

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.580
with stakeholders a lot of these challenges cannot be addressed.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.840
These things are fueling a second shift,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.990
which is the integration of sustainability into core strategy

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.220
as a means to mitigate risks that they're facing,

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:42.035
um, but also to strengthen their resilience in the face of VUCA World.

00:05:42.035 --> 00:05:45.900
And finally, to also capture value

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.225
and- and see the opportunities for creating new value by focusing on sustainability.

00:05:50.225 --> 00:05:52.175
So before we go,

00:05:52.175 --> 00:05:55.260
um, into more detail,

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.189
I want to step back and just say a few words about sustainability,

00:05:58.189 --> 00:06:02.345
because it is a term that is often understood differently by different people.

00:06:02.345 --> 00:06:06.950
Um, it seems to invoke disparate reactions from different communities.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.830
Um, and I think a starting place for thinking about

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.190
sustainability is the framing Jane Jacobs offers,

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.010
when she reminds us that the economy is

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.700
a wholly owned subsidiary of the environment and not the other way around.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.500
We really can't have an economy without an environment,

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.115
all of the inputs come from our environment.

00:06:25.115 --> 00:06:28.880
Um, yet sustainability is really not environmental term,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.810
or a green concept as- as we've tended to think of it in the past.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.525
In fact, it's a very human concept.

00:06:35.525 --> 00:06:38.570
Uh, sustainability is about building a society that

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:41.845
provides for the long-term well-being of its people.

00:06:41.845 --> 00:06:45.980
And this requires us to recognize our fundamental dependence on the natural world,

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.070
and to know that we must generate social,

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:52.325
economic, and environmental value in order to thrive.

00:06:52.325 --> 00:06:57.395
So we like to use the term intergenerational well-being to describe sustainability,

00:06:57.395 --> 00:07:00.110
because we're talking about the well-being of all people,

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.570
not just a privileged few,

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:05.860
and we're talking about building a society where we can provide for that well-being,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.890
not just today, but across generations.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.480
[NOISE] Uh, the online course in our strategies

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.290
for sustainability program includes extensive interviews with Kate Brandt,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:18.115
Google Sustainability Officer and Adam Lowry,

00:07:18.115 --> 00:07:21.385
Co-founder of Method Cleaning Products and Ripple Foods.

00:07:21.385 --> 00:07:24.819
And what I wanted to do is just incorporate throughout this webinar,

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.790
three brief clips, um,

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.375
from those interviews that you'll see in more, uh,

00:07:29.375 --> 00:07:33.710
completion on the online course to give you a few industry perspectives,

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.770
people who are grappling with this in their own companies,

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.900
because so many of you are doing the same.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.680
So let's turn to a- a brief clip and hear their perspective on,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.005
uh, why we're seeing a shift in business approach.

00:07:47.005 --> 00:07:48.650
Business needs to have

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.680
a broader perspective around the innovation that it's providing [MUSIC].

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.860
And at- at a very basic level,

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.085
we're living in a time where a lot of the social contract

00:07:58.085 --> 00:08:01.560
between business and societies is broken down.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.070
And a lot of that, I would argue is because of

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:07.145
a hyper focus on shareholder primacy, right?

00:08:07.145 --> 00:08:11.530
When you put shareholders in front of anyone else, and then,

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:14.600
yeah you've got some policymakers in your back pocket,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.910
then the outcome sometimes are things that aren't good for the population as a whole.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:23.630
And rightfully so, people get angry about that, right?

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:27.740
What I'm trying to do with my businesses and many other people are trying to do is

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.255
try to build in enlightened corporate governance into that model of innovation,

00:08:32.255 --> 00:08:37.525
so that what gets innovated is inherently better for society and environment as well.

00:08:37.525 --> 00:08:39.740
And if- that's a design exercise.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.060
And if you can do that well,

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:45.160
and then you can grow a business focusing not just on one thing,

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:46.305
which is shareholder return,

00:08:46.305 --> 00:08:49.190
but a broader set of things which makes managing

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:52.415
and leading a business more complex and a little harder.

00:08:52.415 --> 00:08:54.915
But if you can do that and do that well,

00:08:54.915 --> 00:08:56.250
then you're achieving the goal.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.430
There is increased motivation for

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:02.090
companies to shift toward sustainability for a few reasons.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.830
I think generally there is now

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.055
an expectation that businesses will serve a social purpose.

00:09:07.055 --> 00:09:09.935
You know, we heard that from Larry Fink in his shareholder letter.

00:09:09.935 --> 00:09:11.480
Uh, that is a tremendous shift.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.610
And I think that's led to, you know,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.310
much more executive engagement from corporate leaders,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:20.135
and philanthropy, and advocating for social environmental causes.

00:09:20.135 --> 00:09:24.230
And I think it's led to a lot more activity within companies are really having

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:29.180
very robust sustainability programs that I think are really geared towards, you know,

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.609
expectations from customers, from employees,

00:09:31.609 --> 00:09:35.360
and from investors, that- that we're seeing increasingly that, you know,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.400
you need to have time-bound goals,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.790
you need to be incorporating this into your operations,

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.480
and increasingly incorporate into

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.810
your business strategy and making it part of how you think about generating value.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.660
I think that today's operating environment is

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:50.680
really requiring businesses to take more of a systems approach,

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:55.280
and I think that's both because we are increasingly facing greater risks.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.140
So looking at changing climate,

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.860
constraints on water, constraints on other natural resources,

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.340
and its sustainability is a really critical way to assess those risks,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.155
to do scenario planning,

00:10:06.155 --> 00:10:10.895
and to develop new strategies in the face of a world that's changing before our eyes.

00:10:10.895 --> 00:10:13.520
Equally, I think there's a lot of opportunity.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.510
You know, when we think about developing more circular business models, uh,

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:19.700
where we're designing waste out of systems,

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.010
that creates tremendous new opportunities,

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.435
new opportunities for value.

00:10:24.435 --> 00:10:28.750
Thanks. So to complement their perspectives,

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:30.850
I wanna just delve in briefly to looking at

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.980
the perspective of various stakeholder groups,

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:35.090
investors, customers, and employees, and see,

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.870
you know, how are they perceiving the role of business now?

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.590
Are there, are we starting to see shifts in that way that might also be contributing to,

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:46.645
uh, the reason that business is becoming to embrace sustainability.

00:10:46.645 --> 00:10:48.955
Uh, so let's, let's turn first to investors.

00:10:48.955 --> 00:10:51.130
You heard Kate Brandt mentioned Larry Fink.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.830
Uh, in 2018, Larry Fink,

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.300
the CEO of BlackRock,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.295
the world's largest investment manager,

00:10:57.295 --> 00:10:59.770
gave a wake-up call to corporate CEOs when he wrote in

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:03.490
his annual letter to BlackRock's investees announcing that,

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:07.150
"A company's ability to manage environmental, social,

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:10.420
and governance matters demonstrates the leadership and

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:14.020
good governance that is so essential to sustainable growth,

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:18.550
which is why we are increasingly integrating these issues into our investment process."

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:20.560
That was, that was a big wake-up call,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.570
uh, for companies, and, and of course,

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:26.440
a lot of companies had already perceived that, uh, shift in,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.975
in requirement and recognized the benefits of, uh,

00:11:28.975 --> 00:11:31.825
reporting on environmental, social, and governance issues.

00:11:31.825 --> 00:11:34.305
If we look at, um, the data,

00:11:34.305 --> 00:11:39.060
85 percent of companies now produce and share ESG reports with investors,

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.520
which is up from only 20 percent in 2011,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.935
that's a pretty big change in a few years,

00:11:43.935 --> 00:11:46.170
and now we've got more data that demonstrates that

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.595
positive ESG performance is correlated,

00:11:49.595 --> 00:11:51.835
uh, with a lower cost of capital,

00:11:51.835 --> 00:11:55.850
better operational performance, and higher stock price.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.800
Let's turn to employees and look at what they're saying.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:03.400
So, uh, they're putting similar pressure on companies,

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.305
younger generations in particular,

00:12:05.305 --> 00:12:08.080
who make up the largest population in the workforce

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.380
expect businesses to do much more than turn a profit.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:14.080
Eighty-three percent of millennials and 80 percent of

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.450
generation Z employees believe

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:20.960
that business success should be measured in terms of more than financial performance.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:26.470
Millennials also believe that employers are generally out of sync with them.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:28.120
When they were asked to identify,

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:31.630
the millennials were asked to identify three priorities for business,

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:36.460
job creation, improving society and enhancing livelihoods,

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.235
and improving the environment rose to the top.

00:12:39.235 --> 00:12:46.255
Um, well, millennials say their company's top three priorities are profit generation,

00:12:46.255 --> 00:12:49.630
driving efficiency, and producing goods.

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:54.890
These happen to be the three lowest priorities on the millennials' list.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:59.830
Customers too see business as responsible for improving society.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:03.610
Eighty-five percent of people surveyed across 20 countries hold

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.645
companies they buy from accountable for improving their lives.

00:13:07.645 --> 00:13:12.130
The same level of expectation they have of government where we traditionally thought,

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.140
um, the institutions we've traditionally thought

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.300
were responsible for improving our lives.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.145
So what we're seeing is,

00:13:18.145 --> 00:13:24.310
as companies start experiencing more and more of the realities of VUCA world,

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:27.775
as well as these heightened expectations of diverse stakeholders,

00:13:27.775 --> 00:13:30.940
they're increasingly integrating new perspectives as

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:33.970
they take the system's approach and look beyond themselves, um,

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:36.280
they're engaging with more diverse stakeholders, uh,

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:40.960
that they now see the deep connection they have with and ultimately

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.150
recognizing that pursuing sustainability can

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:46.285
drive innovation and long-term value creation.

00:13:46.285 --> 00:13:50.665
So here's what we can hear from an industry perspective.

00:13:50.665 --> 00:13:53.530
Method was a business that I started in 2000.

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.810
We raised $25 million of capital.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:58.960
uh, when that business got sold,

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:03.550
it achieved multiples in that sale that were far

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:08.485
beyond businesses that had not filled sustainability into their business models.

00:14:08.485 --> 00:14:10.494
The shareholders, the employees,

00:14:10.494 --> 00:14:14.590
they all made more money financially because the business

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:18.865
we- explicitly because the business was sustainable, not in spite of it.

00:14:18.865 --> 00:14:20.305
The business has since grown,

00:14:20.305 --> 00:14:22.630
the business has since sold again,

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:26.050
and those shareholders have made more money

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:29.515
because of the type of business that Method is,

00:14:29.515 --> 00:14:30.910
not in spite of it.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:34.090
So it's a very real example of shareholder value.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:39.310
I mean, we could just measure it on shareholder primacy alone has been higher because

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:45.010
the business focuses on social outcomes and the environment, not in spite.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:47.050
Thank you so much. So, um,

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:50.290
I just wanna shift now back to Unilever and,

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:53.200
and mention that Paul Polman took quite a similar tact to

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:56.130
the one Adam Lowry is describing, um,

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.380
when he questioned the traditional notion that generating

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.200
profits must be the primary purpose of business,

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.965
ultimately the end, leading to societal benefit.

00:15:04.965 --> 00:15:10.550
Rather, he argued that it was the other way around.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:15.625
By focusing first on improving lives and generating genuinely sustainable solutions,

00:15:15.625 --> 00:15:18.790
companies would be profitable over the long term.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.685
The new operating system for business, he argued,

00:15:21.685 --> 00:15:24.640
would drive growth through innovations that bring

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:29.335
new sustainability benefits to consumers and retailers,

00:15:29.335 --> 00:15:33.740
lower cost by reducing waste and energy use,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.330
and help manage supply chain risk by securing

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.510
long-term sustainable sourcing of materials.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:44.875
And he called for a shift in focus from shareholders,

00:15:44.875 --> 00:15:50.270
just like the shareholder primacy Adam Lowry talks about, to all stakeholders.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:54.640
He says, "I don't think our fiduciary duty is to put shareholders first.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.125
I say the opposite.

00:15:56.125 --> 00:16:00.460
What we firmly believe is that if we focus our company on improving the lives

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:04.780
of the world's citizens and come up with genuine sustainable solutions,

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:08.350
we are more in sync with consumers and society,

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:11.845
and ultimately, this will result in good shareholder returns."

00:16:11.845 --> 00:16:14.755
So he's really flipping our traditional understanding

00:16:14.755 --> 00:16:16.690
and mirroring what we heard from millennials,

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:19.780
which is, a lot of companies just seem to be out of sync with their priorities.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.885
Paul Polman is focusing on getting in sync.

00:16:22.885 --> 00:16:25.600
So let's turn from the 'why',

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.790
our first question, uh,

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:30.490
in this webinar, to the 'what'.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:33.910
What are some of the concrete changes that

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:38.080
organizations are making as they embrace sustainability?

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:42.400
Organizations are changing the way they do business across many areas,

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:47.245
but I'd like to highlight three primary ones here and then provide some examples of each.

00:16:47.245 --> 00:16:52.300
They're adopting business models and practices to focus on sustainability,

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:56.155
they're innovating to create sustainable products and brands,

00:16:56.155 --> 00:16:58.450
and they're also partnering, often,

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:02.810
with competitors to drive scaled impact.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.370
Alongside Method and Google,

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:09.670
there are a few but certainly growing number of companies

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:13.300
experimenting with and adopting circular economy approaches.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.610
Uh, let's take a brief look at a couple of

00:17:15.610 --> 00:17:18.610
examples to illustrate the potential of circular models.

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:20.350
These are just two of many,

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:23.515
uh, but let's start with Michelin.

00:17:23.515 --> 00:17:28.690
Uh, Michelin has transitioned from a product to a service-oriented business model.

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:31.945
By leasing rather than selling tires,

00:17:31.945 --> 00:17:34.690
the company stays engaged with customers because

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:37.030
it has that continuous engagement with them,

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:39.505
rather than just selling your product and saying goodbye,

00:17:39.505 --> 00:17:45.055
and then can gather customer feedback and informed product innovation more efficiently.

00:17:45.055 --> 00:17:49.960
By controlling its products through leasing rather than selling,

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:53.350
good tires can be recovered and released,

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.930
and end of life cycle tires can be

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:59.335
recycled into playgrounds surfaces or converted into fuel.

00:17:59.335 --> 00:18:03.910
The company has 97 percent recycling rates in the EU and Brazil,

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.340
and this is something that's much more possible because it is

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.460
controlling the supply by leasing rather than selling.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.880
Caterpillar has a global network of

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:20.090
re-manufacturing facilities and takes back millions of end of life units.

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.575
That's each year, um,

00:18:23.575 --> 00:18:27.429
which requires 85 to 95 percent less energy than new production,

00:18:27.429 --> 00:18:29.080
and which reduces, of course,

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:32.300
waste and the need for virgin resources.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.515
A growing number of companies such as Eileen Fisher,

00:18:38.515 --> 00:18:39.580
a lot of companies you know,

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:41.440
Warby Parker, Ben and Jerry's,

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:44.755
Etsy, Seventh Generation, Method, you've heard about,

00:18:44.755 --> 00:18:49.075
have become certified benefit corporations or B corporations,

00:18:49.075 --> 00:18:53.410
as a way to operate under a legal requirement to integrate social,

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:56.410
economic, and environmental values and goals.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:19:02.605
B corporations are for profit entities just like C corporations, but they, uh,

00:19:02.605 --> 00:19:05.290
are required legally to meet

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.725
the highest standards of verified social and environmental performance,

00:19:08.725 --> 00:19:13.030
and to consider the impact of their decisions on all stakeholder groups,

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:16.300
not just shareholders, including their workers,

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:20.220
customers, suppliers, community, and the environment.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:26.985
There are now 3,000 B corporations across about 150 industries and 60 corporations.

00:19:26.985 --> 00:19:30.780
Several multinationals, it's quite interesting to see, including;

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:34.515
Procter and Gamble, Unilever, Danone, General Mills,

00:19:34.515 --> 00:19:39.660
and others have recently accelerated their acquisition of the corporations,

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:43.200
um, and they say they're, they're using these sustainability innovators,

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:46.060
these B Corporations, int- integrating them within

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:49.270
their own companies to help accelerate their own learning on environmental,

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:50.515
social, and governance issues,

00:19:50.515 --> 00:19:54.745
to figure out how to drive sustainable product and brand creation.

00:19:54.745 --> 00:19:59.020
Um, and David illustrates the financial benefits of the B Corporation approach.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:00.580
B Corporations averaged to

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:05.290
50 percent revenue growth rate during the 2008 financial crisis and

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:08.530
had a higher revenue growth rate than public firms of

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.920
comparable size throughout the subsequent great recession,

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.595
according to a 2018 study conducted at Yale University.

00:20:15.595 --> 00:20:19.700
Um, let's hear a little bit more from, from Adam and Kate.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:22.840
So both of the businesses that I have started,

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.030
Method products and Ripple Foods are B Corps.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:29.920
Uh, they're both certified B Corps and benefit corporations both in Delaware.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.800
And what's been interesting is the journey.

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:34.555
I think with Method, we,

00:20:34.555 --> 00:20:37.750
we were one of the founding B Corp back in 2007.

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:42.010
I forget exactly where we were on the rating scale which goes from 0-200.

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:45.235
I think we're right around a hundred and or maybe high 90s to begin with.

00:20:45.235 --> 00:20:48.070
The businesses is now at 142, and its,

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.920
I believe the highest if not- if not,

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:56.110
it's one of the highest manufacturing businesses highest rated B Corps in the world,

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:58.240
and a lot of that is a function of some of the things that

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.325
we've been able to do as we've grown.

00:21:00.325 --> 00:21:03.550
The most shining example of that would be building

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:06.550
a sustainable manufacturing facility in the inner city of Chicago

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.715
to make all of Methods products for North America.

00:21:09.715 --> 00:21:14.065
And this is a facility that's renewably powered with utility-scale wind and solar,

00:21:14.065 --> 00:21:15.850
uh, it's water neutral,

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.200
it's landfill-free, it's got

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:20.920
the largest rooftop greenhouse in the world growing leafy greens on its roof,

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:22.795
growing vegetables in a food desert.

00:21:22.795 --> 00:21:24.985
So really cool example.

00:21:24.985 --> 00:21:27.220
Those are the types of things that you can do as you start to

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.410
achieve some scale and you have a bigger balance sheet,

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:32.080
you can do more and more interesting things.

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:34.750
And importantly, those are things that are not

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:37.315
just done so that I can sit here and talk about them.

00:21:37.315 --> 00:21:39.430
Those are things that actually lower

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.775
the cost structure of the manufacturing of the business.

00:21:42.775 --> 00:21:44.950
They improve our, uh,

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:47.800
vacancy rates and truancy rates on,

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.810
uh, line workers at the factory.

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:52.360
They're hard financial metrics that

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:57.025
those sustainability investments or allowing a method to enjoy now,

00:21:57.025 --> 00:21:59.770
am, you know, and that's reflected in a B Corps score,

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:01.660
and that's the way it should be.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:04.720
If you do the right things that should accrued all three bottom lines,

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.060
it should get reflected in how you

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.255
measure yourself from a social and environmental standpoint.

00:22:10.255 --> 00:22:13.270
So let's shift now to sustainable products,

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.120
the third area, I mentioned we- we'd explore.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.940
Companies are recognizing the growing opportunity for

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:23.815
sustainable product innovation as consumers increasingly prefers sustainable options,

00:22:23.815 --> 00:22:30.655
and consumer demand is also moving as the population changes to emerging markets,

00:22:30.655 --> 00:22:34.105
where needs are often related directly to sustainability,

00:22:34.105 --> 00:22:37.465
access to water, sanitation, etc.

00:22:37.465 --> 00:22:41.200
So what we're starting to see is companies are really taking

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.860
off in terms of capturing the value that's possible through sustainable,

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:47.575
um, product and brand innovation,

00:22:47.575 --> 00:22:53.530
but also some interesting efforts to really work collaboratively with partners on

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:56.755
the ground to pair sustainable products and brands

00:22:56.755 --> 00:23:00.850
with campaigns that create positive social and environmental impact.

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:04.510
So let's look at Unilever as an example in India.

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:07.630
Um, the company recognized that cholera, typhoid,

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:11.980
and skin and eye infections were the leading causes of child mortality.

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:16.180
So it designed, it innovated a new product like buoy soap,

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:20.620
which is a water-saving soap because this happens to be in a very arid region,

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:23.830
but also with 10 times the protection against

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:28.015
the specific disease-causing germs that we're the ones causing these,

00:23:28.015 --> 00:23:31.165
these mortal, um, diseases for, for children.

00:23:31.165 --> 00:23:33.670
And then what they did is after innovating the product,

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:36.250
they partner with local governments, NGOs,

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:40.090
and a network of 70,000 rural women to

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:45.040
integrate product marketing and distribution with hand washing campaigns,

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:49.570
um, reaching millions of households and dramatically reducing childhood disease rates.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:55.570
[NOISE] Unilever, um, has developed 26 of these sustainable living brands,

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.460
what they call their sustainable living brands,

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:01.720
which grew 46 percent faster than the rest of the business and delivered

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:06.800
70 percent of the company's overall growth in 2017.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:11.515
Now, um, some challenges are, um,

00:24:11.515 --> 00:24:15.250
certainly too big for one company to tackle alone and

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:19.480
even one sector of corporations to

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:23.605
tackle alone really if they require partnership across civil society,

00:24:23.605 --> 00:24:25.375
government, and the private sector.

00:24:25.375 --> 00:24:26.860
Um, and so what we're starting to see is

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:30.310
more and more companies recognizing this and forging alliances.

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:33.670
Um, Often engaging in pre-competitive problem solving,

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:38.020
um, to shift their sectors towards sustainability for collective benefit.

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:42.385
I'll just give a few examples and these are certainly very complex,

00:24:42.385 --> 00:24:44.080
uh, systems challenges, uh,

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:45.190
with no simple answers,

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.185
but this will give you a sense of,

00:24:47.185 --> 00:24:50.950
of what some companies are doing in partnership with government and civil society.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:53.200
The Consumer Goods Forum, for example,

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:58.125
is a network of 400 retailers and manufacturers across 70 countries.

00:24:58.125 --> 00:25:01.935
They represent four trillion dollars in combined sales,

00:25:01.935 --> 00:25:04.425
and they're working together to reach net,

00:25:04.425 --> 00:25:07.205
uh, zero net deforestation in their supply chains.

00:25:07.205 --> 00:25:10.855
So palm oil has been a big issue that many of you have probably heard about,

00:25:10.855 --> 00:25:15.715
leading to a lot of deforestation of very important rain forests in Indonesia.

00:25:15.715 --> 00:25:19.300
The Consumer Goods Forum and network of retailers there are,

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:22.810
are working with Indonesian government and other international agencies,

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:24.625
civil society, and a range of,

00:25:24.625 --> 00:25:29.800
of palm oil plantations to try to remove deforestation out of the supply chain,

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:31.960
figure out how else to grow palm oil,

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.210
um, that is less, less disruptive.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.520
And because they're, they're working on this together,

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:38.350
no one company necessarily will lose

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:41.365
competitive advantage if they step up first and say okay we're going to do this,

00:25:41.365 --> 00:25:43.825
but the other companies operate businesses usual,

00:25:43.825 --> 00:25:47.455
uh, that, that first leader may lose competitive advantage,

00:25:47.455 --> 00:25:50.980
while doing this together and making a shared commitment and working on the challenge,

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:53.710
it allows for greater innovation and also,

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:57.820
um, creates more incentive for everyone to move in the right direction.

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:00.730
The Marine Stewardship Council is another initiative.

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:03.490
I've worked on the MSC very, um,

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:06.220
intensely about 20 years ago when it was being launched by

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:08.815
Unilever and World Wildlife Fund, and,

00:26:08.815 --> 00:26:13.360
and the vision is really to create an economic incentive for sustainable fishing through

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:16.285
certification and eco-labeling of well-managed fisheries

00:26:16.285 --> 00:26:19.315
so that products from this fisheries would have chain of custody,

00:26:19.315 --> 00:26:21.580
verification, end up in our restaurants or

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.625
grocery stores and being marked with eco-labeling you see there,

00:26:24.625 --> 00:26:27.880
to indicate to all of us that they came from sustainable sources.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.270
And many of you probably know the state of our oceans is dire,

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:35.875
um, global fisheries are- have been collapsing worldwide for, for decades,

00:26:35.875 --> 00:26:40.360
and the MSC offers a similar opportunity to bring government, business,

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:44.560
and civil society together to create an organization that is,

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.065
is driving, uh, for positive change.

00:26:47.065 --> 00:26:50.139
There are currently about 10 percent of fisheries worldwide

00:26:50.139 --> 00:26:53.860
certified as, as MSC sustainable.

00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:57.235
Finally, um, the Sustainable Apparel Coalition

00:26:57.235 --> 00:27:00.130
has created a set of standardized tools to help companies in

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:03.204
the apparel textile and footwear industry

00:27:03.204 --> 00:27:06.910
measure environmental and social impacts across their supply chains,

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:10.915
allowing to achieve much greater transparency and address,

00:27:10.915 --> 00:27:13.555
uh, the unsustainable behavior collectively.

00:27:13.555 --> 00:27:16.810
And as I mentioned none of these efforts is simple nor is any of them

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:20.185
without unintended consequence- consequences and trade-offs.

00:27:20.185 --> 00:27:23.215
Um, companies in the Consumer Goods Forum, for example,

00:27:23.215 --> 00:27:25.810
don't have clear ways to engage the thousands of

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:28.630
smallholder farmers who are growing oil palms,

00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:31.030
yet without them, deforestation will continue.

00:27:31.030 --> 00:27:33.295
So it's really an effort that is,

00:27:33.295 --> 00:27:34.765
is very complex and,

00:27:34.765 --> 00:27:39.970
um, certainly, um, has not yet reached scale in terms of success,

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:42.760
and none of these three, but it is an avenue through

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:46.735
which change can happen and in which systems change can happen,

00:27:46.735 --> 00:27:50.080
um, and we don't really think there was a kind [LAUGHTER] of a way to do it,

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:53.530
it's just important to acknowledge how complex and challenging it is.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:57.100
[NOISE] So let's, um,

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.630
shift now to a bit of the how.

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:01.465
We've talked about why businesses are shifting their approach.

00:28:01.465 --> 00:28:03.550
We're talking- we've talked a bit about some of

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:06.625
the strategic changes organizations are making,

00:28:06.625 --> 00:28:10.015
you know, what exactly are they doing when they embrace sustainability?

00:28:10.015 --> 00:28:12.310
But also, it's really important to think about how

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:14.500
our organizations going about this internally?

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:17.380
How are they implementing these changes and what barriers

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:20.635
are they coming up against as they try to make the shift?

00:28:20.635 --> 00:28:26.815
So, um, let's take a look inside Unilever to explore this.

00:28:26.815 --> 00:28:33.325
Um, in 2010, Unilever launched a 10-year Sustainable Living Plan or SLP,

00:28:33.325 --> 00:28:35.800
covering all aspects of its business,

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:41.515
incorporating all brands and divisions in every country where it works, um,

00:28:41.515 --> 00:28:44.290
and SLP outlined three 10 year goals

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:48.130
aligned with its purpose to make sustainable living commonplace.

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:49.570
So at that time in 2010,

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:53.410
Unilever revisited its purpose and really focusing on,

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:58.975
you know, our corpus as an organization is to make sustainable living commonplace.

00:28:58.975 --> 00:29:01.195
And so they frame these three, um,

00:29:01.195 --> 00:29:04.375
big 10-year goals as part of the Sustainable Living Plan.

00:29:04.375 --> 00:29:07.405
They said they first want to help more than one billion people,

00:29:07.405 --> 00:29:09.430
improve their health and well being.

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:13.315
We have that briefcase of the Lifebuoy soap as an example.

00:29:13.315 --> 00:29:17.560
They said they want to have the environmental footprint of Unilever products.

00:29:17.560 --> 00:29:19.705
And third, they want to source

00:29:19.705 --> 00:29:23.695
100 percent of their agricultural raw materials sustainably,

00:29:23.695 --> 00:29:27.040
and back to the oil palm growers I mentioned,

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:31.690
enhance the livelihoods of millions of people across the value chain, you know,

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:34.630
if- how do you really take responsibility at

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:38.920
that level for your entire value chain all the way down to the growers?

00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:43.900
So, um, they also have taken this very seriously in terms of, um,

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:47.130
establishing targets and, um,

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:50.010
sub goals under each of these major goals.

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:53.310
So, for example under health and hygiene, or under, um,

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:54.735
health and well being,

00:29:54.735 --> 00:29:59.805
they've established goals around health and hygiene and improving nutrition.

00:29:59.805 --> 00:30:01.950
Under reducing environmental impact,

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:05.055
they've set goals and targets around greenhouse gases,

00:30:05.055 --> 00:30:07.710
water use, waste and packaging,

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:10.965
and, uh, in, and a sustainable sourcing.

