as the person who asked the question acknowledges, it's-it's very complex.

00:43:50.065 --> 00:43:51.910
I- one thing that was really important for

00:43:51.910 --> 00:43:54.280
the Marine Stewardship Council was to have the World Wildlife Fund.

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:56.605
You know, WWF panda logo,

00:43:56.605 --> 00:43:58.360
alongside Unilever when the- when

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:00.820
the organization first launched because it gained credibility,

00:44:00.820 --> 00:44:03.400
um, and it held that standard to a very high level.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:07.330
And- and then once the tent widened and many many more NGOs became involved,

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:12.280
those NGOs made sure that that standard stayed high and appropriate.

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:15.190
And many times pushed the MSC saying the standard wasn't high enough

00:44:15.190 --> 00:44:18.250
and the fishery that got certified shouldn't have been certified, and challenged them.

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:21.100
So this is the complexity of the work,

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:23.050
but- but it needs to be done and I think,

00:44:23.050 --> 00:44:25.570
you know, new technology offers some benefit.

00:44:25.570 --> 00:44:29.590
I think we will be able to trace chain of custody more easily now with

00:44:29.590 --> 00:44:34.315
block chain and with other technologies that will help us ensure that um,

00:44:34.315 --> 00:44:38.305
we know about the products that we're buying and we know more about them.

00:44:38.305 --> 00:44:40.330
But yes, great-great question.

00:44:40.330 --> 00:44:42.520
Right. So another question is that, you know,

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:45.700
our participants- participants come from a variety of different companies,

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:48.145
you know, from different industries and at different scales.

00:44:48.145 --> 00:44:50.280
So a lot of times, you know,

00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:51.570
the companies that are getting

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:54.945
the most exposure through these initiatives are the large corporations.

00:44:54.945 --> 00:44:56.940
You know, these are multinational corporations,

00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:58.200
these are very big,

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:02.085
uh, companies that have reached into many different industries and products.

00:45:02.085 --> 00:45:08.070
So how can we get smaller or medium-sized businesses to be more engaged,

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:12.405
or join these initiatives led by these larger corporations and how can,

00:45:12.405 --> 00:45:15.380
you know, small and medium businesses contribute to this effort?

00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:17.470
So when you say join these initiatives,

00:45:17.470 --> 00:45:18.490
what are you referring to?

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:20.140
So for example, with, you know,

00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:23.560
the consumer uh, that- the forums that you've talked about,

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:24.820
you know, with MSC.

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:27.355
You know a-a lot of transect companies that are represented

00:45:27.355 --> 00:45:30.310
in these initiatives are large, multinational corporations.

00:45:30.310 --> 00:45:30.475
Um um.

00:45:30.475 --> 00:45:34.480
So how can small and medium-sized businesses also participate?

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:36.685
Well, I think it's absolutely essential that they do

00:45:36.685 --> 00:45:39.265
because the sector is complex and that,

00:45:39.265 --> 00:45:40.990
you know, well there's a lot of consolidation,

00:45:40.990 --> 00:45:43.480
and a lot of control um,

00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:46.180
and market share within the larger companies,

00:45:46.180 --> 00:45:48.730
the smaller companies are- are essential and play a vital role.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:51.325
And in fact, in the case of the oil palm example,

00:45:51.325 --> 00:45:52.540
it really is, you know,

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:54.085
one of the challenges is,

00:45:54.085 --> 00:45:56.590
what do you do with thousands and thousands of

00:45:56.590 --> 00:45:59.740
farmers who don't have a clear alternative,

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:01.570
and is Unilever going to take care and

00:46:01.570 --> 00:46:03.760
make sure that their farming practices are sustainable,

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:05.020
and their livelihoods are secured,

00:46:05.020 --> 00:46:08.890
because if not, they have only one way to support themselves,

00:46:08.890 --> 00:46:10.510
which is to continue to cut the forest,

00:46:10.510 --> 00:46:16.090
and- and grow either food or- or oil palm for- for- for engaging the cash economy.

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:21.355
So um, it is absolutely essential that-that all scales are engaged and I think,

00:46:21.355 --> 00:46:24.520
you know, some smaller companies can play a leadership role.

00:46:24.520 --> 00:46:27.940
I- I think about Sustainable Harvest Coffee Company and they, you know,

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:31.030
they are a small operator within the huge coffee,

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:34.000
relatively consolidated coffee industry, and they said,

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:35.320
"we're going to have a different model, we're going to have a

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:37.510
relationship coffee model," which is basically,

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:39.925
you know, we are going to take the linear supply chain, and make it circular,

00:46:39.925 --> 00:46:42.970
and we are going to have every grower that we worked

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:47.619
with part of a team that is connected to roasters,

00:46:47.619 --> 00:46:50.560
and bankers and um, retailers,

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:52.690
so that everybody in the supply chain

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:56.220
knows what the reality of doing business is like um,

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.470
for each stakeholder group in the supply chain.

00:46:58.470 --> 00:46:59.820
And- and they have built that model,

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:02.775
they have Let's Talk Coffee events that they host,

00:47:02.775 --> 00:47:06.940
um and they innovate it every year in a coffee growing country.

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:08.710
And now, all the big players are coming,

00:47:08.710 --> 00:47:12.055
McDonald's is coming, you know, Walmart is coming.

00:47:12.055 --> 00:47:14.590
They want to understand- and- and Sustainable Harvest

00:47:14.590 --> 00:47:17.545
created that platform as a very small company that said, "You know,

00:47:17.545 --> 00:47:18.625
we're a big corporation,

00:47:18.625 --> 00:47:19.870
we have a different business model,

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:22.705
we put 50 percent of profit back into farmer training,

00:47:22.705 --> 00:47:24.594
um, and we believe in transparency."

00:47:24.594 --> 00:47:27.310
They will not do business with anyone unless

00:47:27.310 --> 00:47:30.490
every transaction in the entire supply chain is transparent.

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:33.505
So from farmer to roaster and retailer,

00:47:33.505 --> 00:47:36.310
every transaction is transparent and um,

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:38.320
and that's allowed them to build these trusting relationships,

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:40.105
and- and shift the way business is done,

00:47:40.105 --> 00:47:42.070
um, for this segment of the coffee industry.

00:47:42.070 --> 00:47:46.885
So small companies can- can play a very disruptive and- and positive role.

00:47:46.885 --> 00:47:49.615
Right. So we're getting really close to an hour right now,

00:47:49.615 --> 00:47:51.490
we have time for one last question.

00:47:51.490 --> 00:47:55.435
So um, this question specifically concerns kind of your experience and your perspective.

00:47:55.435 --> 00:47:57.100
So Julia, you've worked with,

00:47:57.100 --> 00:47:58.900
you know, a lot of different sectors,

00:47:58.900 --> 00:48:01.960
you've worked on partnerships between government and- and business,

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:05.365
and- and industry with companies of different size.

00:48:05.365 --> 00:48:07.420
So-so do you see, you know,

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:10.300
certain business sectors or certain industries being more

00:48:10.300 --> 00:48:13.705
receptive to this change than others and conversely,

00:48:13.705 --> 00:48:14.950
you know, which industries,

00:48:14.950 --> 00:48:17.920
or which sectors are you seeing the greatest resistance?

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:20.155
Well, it's a great question.

00:48:20.155 --> 00:48:25.990
Um, you know, I think what is interesting is to see

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:32.710
how different issues engage different stakeholders and- and how that happens.

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:35.140
I mean, is it because you get a green-peace campaign and you're

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:37.600
forced to deal with the palm oil challenge,

00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:40.345
which was-was the case for Nestle and Unilever,

00:48:40.345 --> 00:48:41.920
when they launched the video?

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:43.990
Um or is it that, you know,

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:48.040
you have an enlightened leader who really wants to take responsibility and has-has

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:52.555
insight into some of the impacts the company is having or a combination?

00:48:52.555 --> 00:48:54.640
Um, I think about a lot of the um,

00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:56.290
chemical companies and things,

00:48:56.290 --> 00:49:00.010
who are starting to get more and more engaged because of the plastics issue.

00:49:00.010 --> 00:49:02.560
So maybe some traditional industries we thought wouldn't

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:05.500
necessarily engage so much in this issue are really

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:08.170
recognizing as we look at our oceans and they have become

00:49:08.170 --> 00:49:11.740
filled with- with plastic, including micro plastics.

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:14.500
The average seafood consumer has- consumes about

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:17.890
11,000 particles of micro plastics per year.

00:49:17.890 --> 00:49:20.050
Um, so it's not just the plastic we can

00:49:20.050 --> 00:49:22.180
see in the ocean that we know that we're dumping into the ocean,

00:49:22.180 --> 00:49:24.940
but it's now all of the micro plastics that are there.

00:49:24.940 --> 00:49:28.030
And so companies are starting to recognize responsibility,

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:32.980
and um, I'm working right now on an ocean plastics initiative to-to really say,

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:34.090
how do we get all of

00:49:34.090 --> 00:49:37.300
these global actors from throughout the supply chain engaged in this,

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.610
from the chemical companies, um,

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:44.380
to the recycling infrastructure, globally, across nations.

00:49:44.380 --> 00:49:49.060
Um, so I think really it depends how you look at the- the challenge,

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:51.430
but- but everybody- everybody has a stake and

00:49:51.430 --> 00:49:53.980
everybody's connected to the-the problem, and the solution.

00:49:53.980 --> 00:49:56.140
So it's just a matter of, you know,

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:59.530
what incites the initial- initial engagement.

00:49:59.530 --> 00:50:02.620
Great. So we are on top of the hour right

00:50:02.620 --> 00:50:05.275
now and we're going to have to wrap up the Q&amp;A session.

00:50:05.275 --> 00:50:07.090
So again, thank you very much Julia

00:50:07.090 --> 00:50:09.460
today for sharing your knowledge with our participants.

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:12.700
Um, for those of you who have indicated interest in the program,

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.950
our team will definitely be reaching out with more information.

00:50:14.950 --> 00:50:19.330
If you have any pressing questions for Julia or about the program,

00:50:19.330 --> 00:50:22.300
feel free to reach out to our team as well.

